  "id": "DrugBank_DB01114 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpheniramine, a second-generation antihistamine, is linked to Vasomotor Rhinitis through its indirect pathway via Atopic Conjunctivitis. This relationship is supported by the shared indication of Atopic Conjunctivitis in all three paths, suggesting that Chlorpheniramine may act as a mediator in the treatment of allergic rhinitis. The evidence indicates that Chlorpheniramine is associated with the same clinical condition, Vasomotor Rhinitis, through different downstream agents—Quifenadine, Olopatadine, and Acrivastine—each of which is a known antihistamine. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust, though indirect, relationship between Chlorpheniramine and Vasomotor Rhinitis, likely due to its role in modulating allergic responses that manifest as vasomotor symptoms. The mechanism of action is inferred to involve the suppression of histamine-mediated inflammation, which is a common pathway in the treatment of rhinitis.",
